// =====================================================================
//  Queries for the buggy FQ-CoDel model (fqcodel.pm) -- example 3
// =====================================================================
//
//  Bad event Q : flow 5 takes MORE than its fair share of the output while the
//                other queues are backlogged -- it is dequeued more than 3 times
//                under contention (iq5_deqs_bl > 3), hogging the link and thereby
//                STARVING flows 1-4. Checked at end of run (time=TIME_STEPS=14).
//
//  End of run is written as the state predicate `time=14` (= TIME_STEPS) rather
//  than the "done" label: StormPy's program-level SIMULATOR does not report this
//  model's declared label, whereas a relational atom is evaluated directly from
//  the state (and still works for exact model checking). The "done" label is
//  kept in the .pm for reference.
//
//  The correctness expectation FQ-CoDel should meet is fairness: no flow hogs the
//  output, i.e. iq5_deqs_bl <= 3 by end of run. The known FQ-CoDel bug lets
//  adversarial input traffic violate this. Because the traffic (arrivals) is the
//  *nondeterministic* choice in this MDP, LCRL MAXIMISING P[Q] synthesises the
//  worst-case traffic pattern -- an interpretable explanation of how the bug is
//  triggered (which arrivals make FQ-CoDel over-serve flow 5).

// (1) worst-case over-service of flow 5  =  Pmax[Q]
//     (LCRL maximises this; the learned policy is the worst-case traffic pattern)
Pmax=? [ F (time=14 & iq5_deqs_bl>3) ]

// (2) query form
P=? [ F (time=14 & iq5_deqs_bl>3) ]

// (3) best-case / can fairness be guaranteed?  Pmin[Q] = 1 - Pmax[fairness]
Pmin=? [ F (time=14 & iq5_deqs_bl>3) ]

// (4) fairness (safe complement): reach the end with flow 5 NOT over-served.
//     Pmax of this = 1 - Pmin[Q]; LCRL maximising it synthesises a fair schedule.
P=? [ F (time=14 & iq5_deqs_bl<=3) ]
